## TWO FORMATS (do not confuse) — SECTION 6 CHAT WIDTH vs PR PASTE

| Where | Line wrapping | Purpose |
|-------|---------------|---------|
| **Chat section 6** (Cursor only) | Soft-wrap ~72 chars (max 80) | Scroll **down** to read in chat |
| **PR / Sprinklr paste** (`temp_reply.txt`, `reply_*.txt`, write-reply) | **Mail format** — previous paragraph length & layout | What the customer receives |

**Hard split:** Chat soft-wrap is for reading in Cursor only. PR must **not** use that wrapping. Same wording/encoding; normal blank-line paragraphs; fixed signature layout from `reply-salutation.mdc`. On every PR: **rejoin** chat soft-wraps within paragraphs before writing the paste file.

### A) Chat display only (section 6)

1. Soft-wrap so each line is ~72 characters (hard max **80**).
2. Break only at spaces / punctuation — never mid-word.
3. Keep blank lines between paragraphs (salutation → thank-you → body → survey → signature).
4. Exact **wording** and UTF-8 encoding stay identical to the mail version.

### B) PR paste / Sprinklr (mandatory — unchanged mail format)

When writing the reply file for **PR** / `sprinklr-write-reply`:

1. Use **mail formatting** — same as before the chat-wrap rule existed.
2. **Do not** paste the chat soft-wraps (no ~72-column hard line breaks inside paragraphs).
3. Paragraphs = normal email length: one logical paragraph per block, blank line between blocks.
4. Keep the **fixed closing / signature block** line layout from `reply-salutation.mdc` (verbatim wording and its usual line breaks).
5. Same words, same umlauts/UTF-8, same salutation/body/survey/signature order — only undo chat-only soft wraps.
6. Before PR: if section 6 in chat was soft-wrapped for reading, **rejoin** wrapped lines within each paragraph (join with spaces) into mail-format paragraphs, then write that to the reply file.

**Forbidden:** Letting Cursor chat line-wrapping dictate Sprinklr editor formatting.

